e  so what feinstein and harvard said in  their own world is if estrogens provoke  the bleeding that leads to uterine exam  as intim attic uterine cancer is much  more likely to be found in women  receiving estrogens than in those who do  not receive it and they thought that  this could fully explain the association  between estrogen use and endometrial  cancer they refer to this phenomenon as  a certain entire  therefore we have two possible  explanations for the association between  estrogen use and endometrial cancer one  explanation is that estrogens simply  accelerate the diagnosis of existing  cancers and two estrogens actually cause  cancer now these two explains was a very  different implication for clinical  practice and for public health the  question is how how can we distinguish  between these two explanations which one  is responsible for the association  between estrogen use and endometrial  cancer and this is where the debate was  initiated and there were some  discrepancies among investigators that  we are going to discuss now on the one  hand the investigators from Yale they  proposed the following approach they  said well since estrogens make some  women bleed and therefore more likely to  be diagnosed with pre-existing cancer  when we analyze data from these studies  let's restrict the data analysis to  women who bleed whether they're taking  Austrians or not if we do that then all  women in the analysis because they are  they all have bled they all have the  same likelihood of being diagnosed with  cancer if they have an existing cancer  and therefore if we still find an  association between area news and cancer  it must be that estrogens cause cancer  but there were other equally prominent  investigators who disagreed  people like her seljuq Ken Rothman and  Alec Walker from Boston University and  Harvard University said that restricting  to women who bleed was not the solution  they argued that we would find an  association between estrogen and cancer  diagnosis even among women who bleed and  even if estrogens don't cause cancer  as a consequence if that was true then  the approach that was proposed by the  journal investigators could not truly  distinguish between the two possible  explanations we would still have some  form of ascertaining bias and you may be  wondering how it is possible what the  Boston investigator said is seems  counterintuitive because if all women  selected for the analysis the experience  and bleeding then there should be no  association due to differential  ascertainment well we will return to  this question at the end of this lesson  by that time you'll be able to resolve  this problem by yourself
 

